{:edb/db-before-t 1917 :edb/db-after-t 1918 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535230 50 #inst "2026-10-13T06:33:55.317Z" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186054858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483372 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186054862 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483416 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186056107 1075 #edb/ref 17592186056108 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186056107 1075 #edb/ref 17592186056109 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186056107 1075 #edb/ref 17592186056110 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186056107 1075 #edb/ref 17592186056111 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186056107 1075 #edb/ref 17592186056112 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186056107 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482865 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186056107 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482892 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186056107 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482917 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186056107 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483074 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186056107 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483125 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186056107 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483125 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059385 1071 "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x \\in (1, 5)$?" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186059385 1071 "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x \\in (1, 5)?$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059385 1074 "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only parentheses. So, the endpoints are *not* included. \n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}1, 5{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<      }}\\,\\,      x  \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<      }}\\,\\,      5\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059385 1074 "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only parentheses. So, the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}1, 5{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<}}\\,\\, x \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<}}\\,\\, 5\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186059386 1074 "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{\\big(}}} -4, \\sqrt2\\, {\\color{blue}{\\big]}}\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,.$\n\n- The right endpoint $\\sqrt2$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq      }}\\,.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is \n\n$$\n-4 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt  }}\\,\\,   y \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq}}\\,\\, \\sqrt 2.\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059386 1074 "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{\\big(}}} -4, \\sqrt2\\, {\\color{blue}{\\big]}}\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,$.\n- The right endpoint $\\sqrt2$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq}}\\,$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n-4 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,\\, y \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq}}\\,\\, \\sqrt 2\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186059387 1074 "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-2\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}}  \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-2,2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059387 1074 "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-2\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-2,2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186059388 1074 "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $1$ and $4$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}1,4{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4].\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059388 1074 "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $1$ and $4$ *are* included.\n\n$$\n1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}1,4{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4]\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186059826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483356 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483411 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483380 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483383 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483404 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483422 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483384 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059917 1050 420.0 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059958 1071 "The inequality $-10 \\le x \\le 3$ can be expressed in interval notation as" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186059958 1071 "The inequality $-10\\le x\\le 3$ can be expressed in interval notation as" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186059958 1074 "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-10$ and $3$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n-10\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,x\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-10,3]\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [-10,3].\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186060969 1064 "Consider the region below, which represents the inequality $2 < x < 5$.\n\n![](images/fe/feeae0bfa9b29e6f034a3fd33d1e9fbd369840835af2d6588892ee6edc2fa120.png)\n\nWe can represent this inequality using **interval notation** as follows:\n\n$$\n(2,5)\n$$\n\nThe parentheses $(\\phantom{0})$ tell us that the endpoints $x=2$ and $x=5$ are *not* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval does not include the endpoints, we say that it is **open**.\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (2,5)\n$$\n\nThe symbol $\\in$ means \"lies in\" or \"belongs to.\" So, the above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (open) interval $(2,5)$.*\"" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186060969 1064 "Consider the region below, which represents the inequality $2 < x < 5.$\n\n![](images/fe/feeae0bfa9b29e6f034a3fd33d1e9fbd369840835af2d6588892ee6edc2fa120.png)\n\nWe can represent this inequality using **interval notation** as follows:\n\n$$\n(2,5)\n$$\n\nThe parentheses $(\\phantom{0})$ tell us that the endpoints $x=2$ and $x=5$ are *not* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval does not include the endpoints, we say that it is **open**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (2,5).\n$$\n\nThe symbol $\\in$ means \"lies in\" or \"belongs to.\" So, the above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (open) interval $(2,5).$*\"" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186060970 1064 "Some intervals may include one parenthesis and one bracket.\n\nFor example, consider the following interval:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{\\big[}}} 1,4 {\\color{red}{\\big)}}\n$$\n\nTo express this interval as an inequality, we note the following:\n\n- The left bracket $\\,\\color{blue}[\\,$ tells us that left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq}}$ for this endpoint.\n- The right parenthesis $\\,\\color{red})\\,$ tells us that the right endpoint $4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{<}}\\,$ for this endpoint.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq}}\\,\\, x \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,\\, 4\n$$\n\nThe interval can be graphed as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a67872e8193c2bcc249a9a9006acb916909099e4babdbe5cdfa6d2643c10ad2.png)\n\nIntervals with one bracket and one parenthesis are called **half-open** (or **half-closed**)." 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186060970 1064 "Some intervals may include one parenthesis and one bracket. \n\nFor example, consider the following interval:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{\\big[}}} 1,4 {\\color{red}{\\big)}}\n$$\n\nTo express this interval as an inequality, we note the following:\n\n- The left bracket $\\,\\color{blue}[\\,$ tells us that left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq}}$ for this endpoint.\n\n- The right parenthesis $\\,\\color{red})\\,$ tells us that the right endpoint $4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{<      }}\\,$ for this endpoint.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is \n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq  }}\\,\\,   x \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,\\, 4.\n$$\n\nThe interval can be graphed as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a67872e8193c2bcc249a9a9006acb916909099e4babdbe5cdfa6d2643c10ad2.png)\n\nIntervals with one bracket and one parenthesis are called **half-open** (or **half-closed**)." 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186060973 1064 "Now consider the region below, which represents the inequality\n\n$$\n-3 \\leq x \\leq 1\n$$\n\n![](images/38/38c5bf5408bbbeb0f63e198e0564a37e8fe6a34d7f2ce340751d8000443123ed.png)\n\nWe express this inequality using interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-3,1]\n$$\n\nThe brackets $[\\phantom{0}]$ tell us that the endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=1$ *are* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval includes the endpoints, we say that it is **closed**.\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [-3,1]\n$$\n\nThe above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (closed) interval $[-3,1]$.*\"" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186060973 1064 "Now consider the region below, which represents the inequality $-3 \\leq x \\leq 1.$\n\n![](images/38/38c5bf5408bbbeb0f63e198e0564a37e8fe6a34d7f2ce340751d8000443123ed.png)\n\nWe express this inequality using interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-3,1]\n$$\n\nThe brackets $[\\phantom{0}]$ tell us that the endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=1$ *are* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval includes the endpoints, we say that it is **closed**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [-3,1].\n$$\n\nThe above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (closed) interval $[-3,1].$*\"" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186061086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061087 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186061086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061088 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186061086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061089 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186061086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061090 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186061086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061091 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186061086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482836 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186061086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482846 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186061086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482940 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186061086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483097 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186061086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483189 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186061086 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483189 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186061768 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482982 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186061768 1078 #edb/ref 17592186482982 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064002 1071 "Factor the quadratic expression $10a^2 + 11a + 3$." 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064002 1071 "Factor the quadratic expression $10a^2 + 11a + 3.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064002 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n10a^2 + 11a + 3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($10 \\times 3 = 30$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($11$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $30.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $30$ are\n\n$$\n(1,30), \\quad (2,15), \\quad (3,10), \\quad (5,6).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(5,6)$ add to $11{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+6 & =11\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $5a + 6a,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{a}^{2}+11a+3 & = \\\\ 10{a}^{2}+5a+6a+3 & = \\\\ (10{a}^{2}+5a)+(6a+3) & = \\\\ 5a(2a+1)+3(2a+1) & = \\\\ (5a+3)(2a+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064002 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n$10a^2 + 11a + 3$.\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($10 \\times 3 = 30$), and\n- add to the linear coefficient ($11$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $30$.\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $30$ are\n$(1,30), \\quad (2,15), \\quad (3,10), \\quad (5,6)$.\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(5,6)$ add to $11{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 + 6 &= 11✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $5a + 6a$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10a^{2} + 11a + 3 &=  \\\\\n10a^{2} + 5a + 6a + 3 &=  \\\\\n(10a^{2} + 5a) + (6a + 3) &=  \\\\\n5a(2a + 1) + 3(2a + 1) &=  \\\\\n&= (5a + 3)(2a + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064003 1071 "Factor the expression $3x^2 - 16x + 16$." 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064003 1071 "Factor the expression $3x^2 - 16x + 16.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064003 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n3x^2 - 16x + 16.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3 \\times 16 = 48$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-16$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we only need to consider the negative factor pairs of $48.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $48$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-48), \\quad (-2,-24), \\quad (-3,-16), \\quad (-4,-12), \\quad (-6,-8).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-4,-12)$ add to $-16{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)+(-12) & =-16\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-4x - 12x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-16x+16 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x-12x+16 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-4x)-(12x-16) & = \\\\ x(3x-4)-4(3x-4) & = \\\\ (x-4)(3x-4) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064003 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n$3x^2 - 16x + 16$.\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3 \\times 16 = 48$), and\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-16$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we only need to consider the negative factor pairs of $48$.\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $48$ are\n$(-1,-48), \\quad (-2,-24), \\quad (-3,-16), \\quad (-4,-12), \\quad (-6,-8)$.\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-4,-12)$ add to $-16{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-4) + (-12) &= -16✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-4x - 12x$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - 16x + 16 &=  \\\\\n3x^{2} - 4x - 12x + 16 &=  \\\\\n(3x^{2} - 4x) - (12x - 16) &=  \\\\\nx(3x - 4) - 4(3x - 4) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 4)(3x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064004 1071 "Factor the quadratic expression $2c^2 + 15c - 8$." 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064004 1071 "Factor the quadratic expression $2c^2 + 15c - 8.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064004 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n2c^2 + 15c - 8.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times -8 = -16$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($15$).\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of $-16{:}$\n\n$$\n(1,-16),\\quad (-1,16), \\quad (2,-8), \\quad (-2,8), \\quad (4,-4)\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,16)$ add to $15{:}$ \n\n$$\n(-1) + 16 = 15 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, rewrite the linear term as $- c+16c,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{c}^{2}+15c-8 & = \\\\ 2{c}^{2}-c+16c-8 & = \\\\ (2{c}^{2}-c)+(16c-8) & = \\\\ c(2c-1)+8(2c-1) & = \\\\ (2c-1)(c+8) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064004 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n$2c^2 + 15c - 8$.\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times -8 = -16$), and\n- add to the linear coefficient ($15$).\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of $-16{:}$\n$(1,-16),\\quad (-1,16), \\quad (2,-8), \\quad (-2,8), \\quad (4,-4)$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,16)$ add to $15{:}$\n\n$$\n(-1) + 16 = 15 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, rewrite the linear term as $-c+16c$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2c^{2} + 15c - 8 &=  \\\\\n2c^{2} - c + 16c - 8 &=  \\\\\n(2c^{2} - c) + (16c - 8) &=  \\\\\nc(2c - 1) + 8(2c - 1) &=  \\\\\n&= (2c - 1)(c + 8)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064119 1071 "Simplify the product $(x - 3)(x^2 - 2x + 6)$." 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064119 1071 "Simplify the product $(x - 3)(x^2 - 2x + 6).$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064119 1074 "We distribute both terms of the binomial to the trinomial in the second parenthesis. Then, we simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (x - 3)(x^2 - 2x + 6) \\\\ &= x(x^2 - 2x + 6) - 3(x^2 - 2x + 6) \\\\ &= x^3 - 2x^2 + 6x - 3x^2 + 6x - 18 \\\\ &= x^3 - 5x^2 + 12x - 18 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064119 1074 "We distribute both terms of the binomial to the trinomial in the second parenthesis. Then, we simplify.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(x - 3)(x^2 - 2x + 6) &= \\\n\t\tx(x^2 - 2x + 6) - 3(x^2 - 2x + 6) &= \\\n\t\tx^3 - 2x^2 + 6x - 3x^2 + 6x - 18 &= \\\n\t\tx^3 - 5x^2 + 12x - 18\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064120 1071 "Given that  $(3s^2 + 2s - 1)(s^2 + s + 2) = 3s^4 + \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{a}s^3 + \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{b}s^2 + \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{c}s - 2,$ calculate $a+b+c.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064120 1071 "Given that $(3s^2 + 2s - 1)(s^2 + s + 2) = 3s^4 + \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{a}s^3 + \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{b}s^2 + \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{c}s - 2$, calculate $a+b+c$." 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064120 1074 "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3s^{2} + 2s - 1)(s^{2} + s + 2) &=  \\\\\n3s^{2}(s^{2} + s + 2) + 2s(s^{2} + s + 2) + (-1)(s^{2} + s + 2) &=  \\\\\n3s^{4} + 3s^{3} + 6s^{2} + 2s^{3} + 2s^{2} + 4s - s^{2} - s - 2 &=  \\\\\n&= 3s^{4} + 5s^{3} + 7s^{2} + 3s - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMatching up the coefficients, we find that $a=5$, $b=7$, and $c=3$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\na + b + c = 5 + 7 + 3 = 15\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064120 1074 "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{s}^{2}+2s-1)({s}^{2}+s+2) & = \\\\ 3{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+s+2)+2s({s}^{2}+s+2)+(-1)({s}^{2}+s+2) & = \\\\ 3{s}^{4}+3{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}+2{s}^{3}+2{s}^{2}+4s-{s}^{2}-s-2 & = \\\\ 3{s}^{4}+5{s}^{3}+7{s}^{2}+3s-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMatching up the coefficients, we find that $a=5,$ $b=7,$ and $c=3.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\na + b + c = 5 + 7 + 3 = 15.\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064121 1071 "What is the coefficient of $a^2b$ in the product $(a+b)(a^2 + ab + b^2)$?" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064121 1071 "What is the coefficient of $a^2b$ in the product $(a+b)(a^2 + ab + b^2)?$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064121 1074 "We distribute both terms of the binomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(a + b)(a^{2} + ab + b^{2}) &=  \\\\\na(a^{2} + ab + b^{2}) + b(a^{2} + ab + b^{2}) &=  \\\\\na^{3} + a^{2}b + ab^{2} + a^{2}b + ab^{2} + b^{3} &=  \\\\\n&= a^{3} + 2a^{2}b + 2ab^{2} + b^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of $a^2 b$ is $2$." 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064121 1074 "We distribute both terms of the binomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}) & = \\\\ a({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2})+b({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}) & = \\\\ {a}^{3}+{a}^{2}b+a{b}^{2}+{a}^{2}b+a{b}^{2}+{b}^{3} & = \\\\ {a}^{3}+2{a}^{2}b+2a{b}^{2}+{b}^{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of $a^2 b$ is $2.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483430 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483365 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483415 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064249 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483361 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483405 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064277 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483417 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064277 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483427 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064278 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483375 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064278 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483394 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064448 1050 660.0 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064458 1050 960.0 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064476 1071 "$6{w}^{2}+19w+10=$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064476 1071 "What is $6w^{2} + 19w + 10$?" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064476 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n6{w}^{2}+19w+10.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($6\\times 10=60$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($19$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $60.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $60$ are\n\n$$\n(1,60),\\,(2,30),\\,(3,10),\\,(4,15),\\,(5,12),\\,(6,10).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(4,15)$ add to $19:$ \n\n$$\n4+15=19\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $4w+15w,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{w}^{2}+19w+10 & = \\\\ 6{w}^{2}+4w+15w+10 & = \\\\ (6{w}^{2}+4w)+(15w+10) & = \\\\ 2w(3w+2)+5(3w+2) & = \\\\ (3w+2)(2w+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483263 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483338 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064616 1071 "Factor the following quadratic expression.\n\n$4{x}^{2}-15x+9=(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)($\n{{field-2}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186064616 1071 "Factor the following quadratic expression.$4x^{2} - 15x + 9 =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186064616 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-15x+9.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($4\\times 9=36$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-15$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we only need to consider the negative factor pairs of $36.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $36$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-36),\\,(-2,-18),\\,(-3,-12),\\,(-4,-9),\\,(-6,-6).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-3,-12)$ add to $-15:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)+(-12) & =-15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-3x-12x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-15x+9 & = \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-3x-12x+9 & = \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-3x)-(12x-9) & = \\\\ x(4x-3)-3(4x-3) & = \\\\ (x-3)(4x-3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186065375 1064 "To multiply a binomial and a trinomial, we take each term of the binomial and multiply it by every term in the trinomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\nFor example, to multiply $({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4)$, we multiply ${\\color{blue}{x}}$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4)$, and then we add this to the product of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)(x^{2} + 2x + 4) &=  \\\\\nx(x^{2} + 2x + 4) + 3(x^{2} + 2x + 4) &=  \\\\\n&= x^{3} + 2x^{2} + 4x + 3x^{2} + 6x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we group the like terms and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} + 2x^{2} + 4x + 3x^{2} + 6x + 12 &=  \\\\\nx^{3} + 2x^{2} + 3x^{2} + 4x + 6x + 12 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{3} + 5x^{2} + 10x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186065375 1064 "To multiply a binomial and a trinomial, we take each term of the binomial and multiply it by every term in the trinomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\nFor example, to multiply $({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4),$ we multiply ${\\color{blue}{x}}$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4),$ and then we add this to the product of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)({x}^{2}+2x+4) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}+2x+4)+3({x}^{2}+2x+4) & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4x+3{x}^{2}+6x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we group the like terms and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4x+3{x}^{2}+6x+12 & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}+4x+6x+12 & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+10x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186065449 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned how to factor quadratic trinomials whose quadratic coefficient equals one. In this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include cases where the quadratic coefficient is a positive integer that's different from one.\n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}x^2+{\\color{purple}{7}}x+{\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the quadratic coefficient equals ${\\color{red}{2}}.$\n\nTo factor this trinomial, we must find two numbers that \n\n- *multiply* to give the *product* of the quadratic coefficient and the constant $({\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = 6),$ and\n\n- *add* to give the linear coefficient $({\\color{purple}{7}})$.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:**  We start by writing down the factor pairs of $6{:}$\n\nThe factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(1,6), \\qquad (2,3).\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:**  Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{7}})\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+6 & =7 & ✓ \\\\ 2+3 & =5 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:**  Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=x+6x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+7x+3 & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x+6x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 4:**  Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+x+6x+3 & = \\\\ x(2x+1)+3(2x+1) & = \\\\ (2x+1)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! \n\nWe can check that our answer is correct by expanding the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+1)(x+3) & = \\\\ 2x\\cdot (x+3)+1\\cdot (x+3) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6x+x+3 & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+7x+3 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186065449 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned how to factor quadratic trinomials whose quadratic coefficient equals one. In this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include cases where the quadratic coefficient is a positive integer that's different from one.\n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}x^2+{\\color{purple}{7}}x+{\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nNotice that the quadratic coefficient equals ${\\color{red}{2}}$.\n\nTo factor this trinomial, we must find two numbers that\n\n- *multiply* to give the *product* of the quadratic coefficient and the constant $({\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = 6)$, and\n- *add* to give the linear coefficient $({\\color{purple}{7}})$.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** We start by writing down the factor pairs of $6{:}$The factor pairs of $6$ are\n$(1,6), \\qquad (2,3)$.\n\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{7}})$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 6|= 7 & ✓ \\\\ 2 + 3|= 5 & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=x+6x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} + 7x + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 2x^{2} + x + 6x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$2x^{2} + x + 6x + 3|=; x(2x + 1) + 3(2x + 1)|=; (2x + 1)(x + 3)$.\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWe can check that our answer is correct by expanding the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x + 1)(x + 3) &=  \\\\\n2x \\cdot (x + 3) + 1 \\cdot (x + 3) &=  \\\\\n2x^{2} + 6x + x + 3 &=  \\\\\n2x^{2} + 7x + 3 &= ✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186065450 1064 "To factor a quadratic expression, we must find a factor pair of the *product* of the quadratic coefficient and the constant that adds to the linear coefficient.\n\nWith that in mind, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}y^2 {\\color{purple}{\\,-\\,7}}y + {\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nNotice that,\n\n- the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant, ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = 6$, is *positive*, while\n\n- the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{-7}})$ is *negative*.\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $6.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-6), \\quad (-2,-3).\n$$\n\nFor each pair, their *product* is positive, and their sum is *negative*, which is what we want.\n\nNow, we determine which of these factor pairs sums to the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{-7}}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-6) & =-7\\,✓ \\\\ (-2)+(-3) & =-5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-y - 6y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-7y+3 & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-y-6y+3 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}-y)-(6y-3) & = \\\\ y(2y-1)-3(2y-1) & = \\\\ (y-3)(2y-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186065450 1064 "To factor a quadratic expression, we must find a factor pair of the *product* of the quadratic coefficient and the constant that adds to the linear coefficient.\n\nWith that in mind, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}y^2 {\\color{purple}{\\,-\\,7}}y + {\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nNotice that,\n\n- the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant, ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = 6$, is *positive*, while\n- the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{-7}})$ is *negative*.\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $6$.\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $6$ are\n$(-1,-6), \\quad (-2,-3)$.\nFor each pair, their *product* is positive, and their sum is *negative*, which is what we want.\n\nNow, we determine which of these factor pairs sums to the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{-7}}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-1) + (-6) &= -7✓ \\\\\n(-2) + (-3) &= -5\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-y - 6y$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y^{2} - 7y + 3 &=  \\\\\n2y^{2} - y - 6y + 3 &=  \\\\\n(2y^{2} - y) - (6y - 3) &=  \\\\\ny(2y - 1) - 3(2y - 1) &=  \\\\\n&= (y - 3)(2y - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186073676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483369 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186073705 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483420 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186100937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483370 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186118304 1078 #edb/ref 17592186118308 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186119845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483398 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186119850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483371 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186120365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186120365 1071 "Determine the dimension of $Span{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}$." 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186120365 1071 "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186120365 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the