gned}2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $\\mathbf{u}(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 0\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=2t$ and $\\text{d}v=e^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=2\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(2\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}-\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(2t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, the integrand is zero, so the integral is zero.\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(2t-1) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166526 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}_c(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n![](images/e8/e8db6a28a4671312259595de0609cefb8db0d5fc441e122885335e1165e0cd22.png)\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^