 the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-9 & =-5 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =4 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, ![](images/c5/c57b6f1ce1762add9b5b5d3e15a6363f009482b12b12366434c090d6a7478c6b.svg)" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166590 1074 "We need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\nx_1 B_1 +x_2 B_2 = A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & -4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n![](images/eb/ebd814bcb627ef3b9208bc0960bdaf48b9448396ed7e95d18d8c4abbf1169244.png)\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 18 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=18 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get $x_2 =9.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-9 & =-5 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =4 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, ![](images/df/dfc845ca1739c329e5636726466c76efee7eba19df4adc0909110506f894e72d.png)" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186166591 1074 "We need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\nx_1 p_1(t)+x_2 p_2(t) = q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(5+{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(3t-2{t}^{2}) & =5-3t+3{t}^{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}t+({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =5-3t+3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n![](images/27/271f2b051a53f7aeae9a20ef23b97c9c903a7815f228dfa3ee23e2207bd24ee9.svg)\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations, we get $x_1=1$ and $x_2 =-1.$ Therefore, ![](images/1f/1fc60fff97ff92bd1cc515e6c984fbacb435dcf0fae24e751842eb8daf3390e8.svg)" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166591 1074 "We need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\nx_1 p_1(t)+x_2 p_2(t) = q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(5+{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(3t-2{t}^{2}) & =5-3t+3{t}^{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}t+({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =5-3t+3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n![](images/19/195a50f130cc40c4a725066035f2da3ef25b725df96601e53f70716bd82b4f4a.png)\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations, we get $x_1=1$ and $x_2 =-1.$ Therefore, ![](images/9f/9f23f71e833d7a7f81f511e1dfb72861ed753cb514de43d6cabfeb3c6b65fadb.png)" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186166751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483389 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483390 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483346 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483409 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483363 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483426 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483348 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483412 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483347 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483351 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483388 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483355 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483418 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483397 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483399 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483382 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166982 1050 420.0 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166985 1050 1500.0 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186166996 1050 840.0 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186167059 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186167059 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = (2x - 3y)^{2}$.\nUsing a substitution of the form $z = 2x - 3y$, transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) = f(z)$. What is the function $f(z)$?" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186167059 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(2x-3y)^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=2x-3y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186167059 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-3y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-3\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-3{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2-3{z}^{2}.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186167185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164135 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186167185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164136 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186167185 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-10{e}^{-2t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186167185 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $-10{e}^{-2t}$ |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186167263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186167263 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}6t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$36t-42$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186167263 1071 "$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t) + \\begin{bmatrix}6t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t} + \\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $36t - 42$ |  | . |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186167263 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 3{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot 3{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 12t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-3t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 12t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -3t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 12t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -3t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-3t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=12t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=12\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 12t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =12t\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int 12\\cdot {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12t{e}^{t}-12{e}^{t} \\\\  & =12{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 3{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ 12{e}^{t}(t-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}6(1-2t)+48(t-1) \\\\ 9(1-2t)+48(t-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}36t-42 \\\\ 30t-39\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186167580 1061 30910 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186167580 1061 653397 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186167581 1061 30911 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186167581 1061 653398 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186167582 1061 30915 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186167582 1061 653401 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186167583 1061 30917 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186167583 1061 653404 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186167584 1061 30920 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186167584 1061 653407 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186167991 1064 "Consider the system of ordinary differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) =\nA\n\\mathbf{x}(t) +\n\\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix and $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is a forcing vector.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll derive the formula for the particular solution of the above system using the method of variation of parameters.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_c(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{c}$ is a vector of constants, and $\\Phi$ is the fundamental matrix.\n\nAs usual, for the method of variation of parameters, we'll look for a particular solution of our inhomogeneous system by varying the constant parameters, i.e., in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined.\n\nDifferentiating $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}^{'}(t) & =(Φ\\,u(t))^{'} \\\\  & =Φ{u}^{'}(t)+{Φ}^{'}u(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a solution, substituting it back into the initial system gives a true equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{'}(t)+{Φ}^{'}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, $\\Phi' = A \\Phi.$ Substituting this into the equation simplifies it to the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ{u}^{'}(t)+AΦu(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{'}(t) & =f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\Phi$ is invertible, we can pre-multiply both sides by $\\Phi^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{'}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate both sides of the final equations (ignoring the constant term):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{'}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\displaystyle\\int \\Phi^{-1}\\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186167991 1064 "Consider the system of ordinary differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) =\nA\n\\mathbf{x}(t) +\n\\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix and $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is a forcing vector.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll derive the formula for the particular solution of the above system using the method of variation of parameters.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_c(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{c}$ is a vector of constants, and $\\Phi$ is the fundamental matrix.\n\nAs usual, for the method of variation of parameters, we'll look for a particular solution of our inhomogeneous system by varying the constant parameters, i.e., in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined.\n\nDifferentiating $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =(Φ\\,u(t))^{′} \\\\  & =Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a solution, substituting it back into the initial system gives a true equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, $\\Phi' = A \\Phi.$ Substituting this into the equation simplifies it to the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ{u}^{′}(t)+AΦu(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t) & =f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\Phi$ is invertible, we can pre-multiply both sides by $\\Phi^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate both sides of the final equations (ignoring the constant term):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\displaystyle\\int \\Phi^{-1}\\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186168064 1064 "We've already seen how to write the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf v\\in \\mathbb R^n$ relative some general basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\mathbb R^n.$ In this lesson, we will generalize this concept to abstract vector spaces.\n\nAs an example, consider the matrices $A_1$ and $A_2,$ given by\n\n![](images/28/2828c037a750f078b4d98b93248e800ec05d4899d7eb390dd135326314c44e70.svg)\n\nThe set $\\mathcal{B}=\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of the vector space $V = \\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}.$ Now, suppose that the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are \n\n![](images/2d/2db3ab1e4452eff2f19151cc87a805452433c7f305a80e5480c25241cba42c38.svg)\n\nLet's find the matrix $C$ using this information. \n\nAs usual, any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\nx_1 A_1  + x_2 A_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1,x_2$ are the coordinates of the vector relative to the basis. \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n![](images/64/6489e130a9237881664e1649cc5408cdff87cfb12dcf44c0342b9961cdf7bc81.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 12 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 10 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186168064 1064 "We've already seen how to write the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf v\\in \\mathbb R^n$ relative some general basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\mathbb R^n.$ In this lesson, we will generalize this concept to abstract vector spaces.\n\nAs an example, consider the matrices $A_1$ and $A_2,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe set $\\mathcal{B}=\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of the vector space $V = \\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}.$ Now, suppose that the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nLet's find the matrix $C$ using this information. \n\nAs usual, any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\nx_1 A_1  + x_2 A_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1,x_2$ are the coordinates of the vector relative to the basis. \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 12 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 10 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186168065 1064 "Suppose we fix a basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ of a finite-dimensional vector space $V$ with scalars from $\\mathbb{R}$ (note that \"finite-dimensional\" means that the basis contains a finite number of elements). Then, we can represent any element $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ using the coordinates of $\\mathbf{v}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c9/c9297a30f87baf08295a752cb23f2d314139508f875b4ffa2fffc8763656bd54.svg)\n\nThis defines a bijective mapping $\\phi: V\\to \\mathbb{R}^n.$ Moreover, this particular mapping has some nice properties:\n\n- $\\phi(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}) = \\phi(\\mathbf{u}) + \\phi(\\mathbf{v})$ for any $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in V,$ and\n\n- $\\phi(\\alpha \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\phi(\\mathbf{v})$ for any $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nBecause of this, we say that $V$ is **isomorphic** to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Intuitively, this means that $V$ and $\\mathbb R^n$ have identical structures.\n\nSo, any finite-dimensional vector space is isomorphic to $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for some particular $n.$ This means that instead of studying all possible vector spaces, we can simply study only one family of vector spaces, namely $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for $n\\geq 1.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186168065 1064 "Suppose we fix a basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ of a finite-dimensional vector space $V$ with scalars from $\\mathbb{R}$ (note that \"finite-dimensional\" means that the basis contains a finite number of elements). Then, we can represent any element $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ using the coordinates of $\\mathbf{v}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis defines a bijective mapping $\\phi: V\\to \\mathbb{R}^n.$ Moreover, this particular mapping has some nice properties:\n\n- $\\phi(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}) = \\phi(\\mathbf{u}) + \\phi(\\mathbf{v})$ for any $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in V,$ and\n\n- $\\phi(\\alpha \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\phi(\\mathbf{v})$ for any $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nBecause of this, we say that $V$ is **isomorphic** to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Intuitively, this means that $V$ and $\\mathbb R^n$ have identical structures.\n\nSo, any finite-dimensional vector space is isomorphic to $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for some particular $n.$ This means that instead of studying all possible vector spaces, we can simply study only one family of vector spaces, namely $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for $n\\geq 1.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186168103 1064 "Suppose we have a differential equation of the form \n\n$$\ny ' = f ( a x + b y ),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are real constants. In this case, we can rewrite the equation as a separable differential equation using the substitution $z= ax+by.$ We then proceed to solve this separable equation.\n\n To see how we apply this method, let's consider the following example: \n\n$$\ny' = (x+y)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 1 + \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = g(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = z^2+ 1.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}z & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the general solution to the equation in terms of $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arctan {(x+y)}  = x + C\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186168103 1064 "Suppose we have a differential equation of the form \n\n$$\ny ' = f ( a x + b y ),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are real constants. In this case, we can rewrite the equation as a separable differential equation using the substitution $z= ax+by.$ We then proceed to solve this separable equation.\n\n To see how we apply this method, let's consider the following example: \n\n$$\ny' = (x+y)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 1 + \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = g(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = z^2+ 1.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\te