isible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute $3$ into the blank, we get $66\\,333\\,930,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $30+3=33,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute $9$ into the blank, we get $66\\,333\\,990,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $30+9=39,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186179799 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483354 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186179800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483358 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186179800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483393 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186189778 1078 #edb/ref 17592186189779 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186189784 1078 #edb/ref 17592186189789 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186192795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483424 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186192796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483374 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186192796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483408 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186192982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186192982 1071 "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, X_{3}, X_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}∼B(5, θ)$ with unknown probability of success $θ$. You're given the particular sample\n$x_{1} = 5, x_{2} = 3, x_{3} = 2, x_{4} = 1$.\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n$l(θ) = \\ln a + b\\ln θ + c\\ln (1 - θ)$.\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535230 17592186045417 false] [17592186192982 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(5,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=5,\\,{x}_{2}=3,\\,{x}_{3}=2,\\,{x}_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186192982 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=5$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{5-x},\\,x=0,1,…,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{4}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 10\\cdot 10\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =500,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=5+3+2+1=11.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(500)+11\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5\\cdot 4-11)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(500)+11\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=500+11+9=520.\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186192983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186192983 1071 "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, X_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}∼Po(θ)$ with unknown rate parameter $θ$. You're given the particular sample\n$x_{1} = 3, x_{2} = 4, x_{3} = 2$.\nThe log-likeli