7592186045417 true] [17592186483345 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ above. If the normal equation corresponding to the system $Ax=b$ is given below, what is the value of $a-b-c?$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}18 & 1 & 36 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ b & 2 & a\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483345 1072 #uuid "0200c257-e4df-57c1-8eaf-e14f946ede8a" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483345 1074 "The normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}18 & 1 & 36 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 36 & 2 & 72\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na=72,\\,b=36,\\,c=20,\n$$\n\nand hence\n\n$$\na-b-c=72-36-20=16.\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483332 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483346 1069 "q-106761" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483346 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -18 \\\\ 15 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483346 1072 #uuid "04ab001d-eb12-502f-a292-54d2ac24e44a" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483346 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -18 \\\\ 15 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -18 \\\\ 15 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-18 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -18 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -18 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 15\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ -\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=-3.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =9 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(-3) & =9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6+(-3)=3.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483244 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483347 1069 "q-149722" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483347 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{{e}^{y-x}}{x-y+1}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483347 1072 #uuid "0ebc5c12-2f6e-589d-af5a-42a020eb709e" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483347 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-z}}{z+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{-z}}{z+1}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+1){e}^{z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int (z+1){e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z+1 & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{z} & \\,⟹\\,v={e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (z+1){e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ (z+1){e}^{z}-\\int {e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ (z+1){e}^{z}-{e}^{z} & =x+C \\\\ z{e}^{z} & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-y){e}^{x-y}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=(x-y){e}^{x-y}.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483331 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483348 1069 "q-149712" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483348 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{1}{(3x-y)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=3x-y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483348 1072 #uuid "10277519-c835-51d7-9d38-5cc5b84c2733" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483348 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483264 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483349 1069 "q-110936" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483349 1071 "|  | $Y=3$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ |\n\nCompute $P((X,Y)\\in \\{(2,3),(2,6)\\})$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483349 1072 #uuid "1430584e-5e6b-5a21-98c2-3433956718bf" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483349 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P((X,Y)\\in \\{(2,3),(2,6)\\}) & =P((X,Y)=(2,3))+P((X,Y)=(2,6)) \\\\  & =f(2,3)+f(2,6) \\\\  & =0.25+0.5 \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483302 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483350 1069 "q-332691" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483350 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=3{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483350 1072 #uuid "178b0d68-b124-5a5b-940e-16d447e5211f" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483350 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $3{e}^{-5x},$ and ${e}^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x})+5\\alpha x{e}^{-5x} & =3{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =3{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =3{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x{e}^{-5x}.\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483250 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483351 1069 "q-149723" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483351 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+2}-1.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{x+C}{x+y},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483351 1072 #uuid "1808d5b9-dae6-5dea-b5bd-62c618289147" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483351 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2)\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2 & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1 & \\,⟹\\,v=z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2)\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2)-\\int \\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2)-z & =x+C \\\\ z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+1) & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1) & =x+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1 & =\\frac{x+C}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483305 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483352 1069 "q-149799" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483352 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\,?$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483352 1072 #uuid "22b679e5-bd2f-5f85-b6e8-97dc0feb70a5" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483352 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=(1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\theta {e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{((1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }+(1+\\theta )^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{e}^{2\\theta }({\\theta }^{2}+1+2\\theta +{\\theta }^{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483282 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483353 1069 "q-162776" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483353 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=10x-2\\sqrt{15}y\\,$ on the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483353 1072 #uuid "27a9cc77-ffaa-5079-b6d3-f595b1244e8b" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483353 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨10,-2\\sqrt{15}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨10x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}10=10\\lambda x \\\\ -2\\sqrt{15}=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=5$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{15}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5+15}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-20\\,$ | $\\,20\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-20.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483310 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483354 1069 "q-173783" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483354 1071 "Expressed as an improper fraction in its simplest form,  $(-6)÷\\frac{12}{5}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483354 1072 #uuid "2ad85a11-bba5-5fae-afa8-bc7f058da66c" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483354 1074 "Dividing a negative number by a positive number gives a negative number. Hence, the result will be negative.\n\nTherefore, since \n\n$$\n6÷\\frac{12}{5}=\\frac{6\\times 5}{12}=\\frac{30}{12}=\\frac{5}{2},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n(-6)÷\\frac{12}{5}=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483272 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483304 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483320 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483328 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483355 1069 "q-336714" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483355 1071 "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-1}} both sides of  the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-4}} both sides of the equation." 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483355 1072 #uuid "2f885aae-2228-5978-9940-05380822e48d" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483355 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ To get $\\text{L2},$ we are $\\text{differentiating}$ with respect to $t$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =(Φu(t))^{′} \\\\ {x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L2},$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Let's substitute ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$ from $\\text{L1}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ into the initial system of ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}\\text{ and }\\text{L2}$ into the initial system of ODEs.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $Φ$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{pre-multiplying by }{Φ}^{-1}$ both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483299 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483356 1069 "q-1256" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483356 1071 "The inequality $-3<x<-1$ can be expressed in interval notation as" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483356 1072 #uuid "2f96fab2-a0f2-58ca-be0a-3d7653559b4b" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483356 1074 "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"less than\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-3$ and $-1$ are *not* included. \n\n$$\n-3\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n(-3,-1)\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (-3,-1).\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483252 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483357 1069 "q-166804" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483357 1071 "Expressed as a single fraction, the exact area of the plot of land is$\\,${{field-1}}\nhectares." 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483357 1072 #uuid "319f568f-1d7f-56e6-aaee-eed8bdcd4507" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483357 1074 "The exact area of land is ${(\\frac{7}{9})}^{2}$ hectares.\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{7}{9})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{7}{9})}^{2} & ={{\\frac{7}{9}\\times \\frac{7}{9}}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\times 7}{9\\times 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{7}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{49}{81}.\n$$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483255 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483358 1069 "q-128060" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483358 1071 "Find the value of $(-\\frac{3}{8})÷\\frac{1}{6}.$" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483358 1072 #uuid "35226f58-033d-50fc-9ed5-94472de795ca" 13194139535230 17592186045417 true] [17592186483358 1074 "Dividing a negative number by a positive number gives a negative 